  "translation": {
    "literal": "Red beans grow in the southern land; when spring comes, how many branches do they put forth? I wish you would pluck many of them, for this is the thing that most calls up longing.",
    "poetic": "In the south the red beans grow, and with every spring they send out new shoots. Gather them by the handful, my friend, for nothing speaks of longing like these."
  },
  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "red-bean",
      "kind": "interpretation",
      "title": "The red bean as a love token",
      "body": "紅豆 here means a bright red seed (often called 相思子, \"longing seed\") that Chinese tradition treats as a token of love and absence. Botanists disagree about which southern plant is meant. In the poem the seed stands for the memory of someone far away, so the final line can name the feeling directly: 相思."
    },
    {
      "id": "variants",
      "kind": "text-variant",
      "title": "Variants: 春來 / 秋來 and 願君 / 勸君",
      "body": "Texts differ. Some give 秋來發幾枝 (autumn) instead of 春來, and 勸君休採擷 (I urge you not to pick them) instead of 願君多採擷. The first is the form taught in schools today. Some old collections also print the poem under the title 江上贈李龜年."
    },
    {
      "id": "rhyme",
      "kind": "rhyme",
      "title": "Rhyme: 枝 · 思",
      "body": "Lines two and four end in 枝 (zhī) and 思 (sī), in the same 支 rhyme. The first and third lines end in 國 and 擷, which do not rhyme."
    },
    {
      "id": "poet",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "Wang Wei (699?–759)",
      "body": "Wang Wei was a poet, painter and musician at the Tang court, later called the 詩佛 (the Buddha of poetry) for his Buddhist themes. Su Shi wrote of his work: \"there are paintings in his poems, and poems in his paintings.\""
    }
  ],
